<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sukarno</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Soekarno, Bung Karno</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sukarno was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-independence">Indonesian independence</a> movement and the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. He proclaimed independence on 17 August 1945, led the republic through the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and the short-lived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of Indonesia, and from 1959 ruled through Guided Democracy. He was sidelined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> after 1965 and removed in 1967.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sukarno.jpg" alt="Sukarno" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Onbekend/Anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Presiden_Sukarno.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Governor-generals such as van Heutsz (1904–1909) combined military pacification with administrative reform, but political parties that demanded independence were repressed, and leaders including <b>Sukarno</b>, Mohammad Hatta and Sutan Sjahrir were exiled to remote islands or the Boven-Digoel camp in New Guinea in the 1930s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Organisations such as Budi Utomo (1908), Sarekat Islam and the Indonesian National Party of <b>Sukarno</b> gave the colony's many peoples a common political language, and at the Youth Pledge of 1928 delegates adopted one nation, one people and one language, Indonesian, a form of Malay.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its first test was the Confrontation of 1963–1966, when Indonesia under <b>Sukarno</b> sent raiders across the Borneo border and landed small parties on the peninsula; Commonwealth forces, including British Gurkha battalions, defeated the incursions in a largely secret jungle war, and the conflict ended after Suharto's rise with…</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bandung Conference of Asian and African states, hosted by Indonesia’s president <b>Sukarno</b> in 1955, adopted a declaration that included them and a collective pledge to stay neutral in the Cold War.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>It has evoked grandeur and pride for most Indonesians: President <b>Sukarno</b> made a point of showing it to foreign dignitaries, the Suharto government restored it with UNESCO's help, and it has come to stand, with wayang and gamelan, for a classical Javanese past.</blockquote><a href="/work/borobudur">Borobudur · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sukarno</b> found his first recording, made in early 1951 with the orchestras of Radio Republik Indonesia, too ornate and asked for something as plain as the red and white flag and as grave as the Dutch anthem &quot;Wilhelmus&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/indonesia-raya">Indonesia Raya</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Surabaya, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-7.25,112.74&amp;year=1945" aria-label="Surabaya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7670 2434 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sukarno"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8120 2704h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8120 2704h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-7.25,112.74&amp;year=1945">What was here in 1945? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
